  "slides": [
    {
      "page_title": "Why Kafka Needs Retention",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Log, Not Queue",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Topic = append-only distributed commit log",
            "Consuming never deletes — unlike a queue",
            "Offset is a read cursor, not a delete ack",
            "100 consumers read the same bytes, stored once"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Retention is the only mechanism that reclaims disk in Kafka. Without it a broker fills its disk and goes offline."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Two Policies"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "`cleanup.policy`",
            "Keeps",
            "Fits"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "`delete`",
              "Recent window",
              "Events, logs"
            ],
            [
              "`compact`",
              "Latest per key",
              "State, config"
            ],
            [
              "`compact,delete`",
              "Latest, bounded",
              "Changelogs"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Default is `delete` with `retention.ms=604800000` (7 days)",
            "Retention is per-partition, per-broker — never per-topic",
            "Footprint = sum over partitions × replication factor",
            "A background thread enforces it, not the write path"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Broker vs Topic"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Level",
            "Config",
            "Wins?"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Broker",
              "`log.retention.hours`",
              "Fallback"
            ],
            [
              "Broker",
              "`log.retention.bytes`",
              "Fallback"
            ],
            [
              "Topic",
              "`retention.ms`",
              "Always"
            ],
            [
              "Topic",
              "`retention.bytes`",
              "Always"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "kafka-configs.sh --alter \\",
            "  --entity-type topics --entity-name t \\",
            "  --add-config retention.ms=86400000  # 1 d"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Sizing The Disk"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "peak throughput × window × RF × (1 + headroom)",
            "Headroom of 30-40% is standard, not optional",
            "Expect lag between 'expired' and 'actually deleted'"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "text",
          "lines": [
            "50 MB/s x 86400 s x 7 d x RF 3",
            "= ~90 TB across the cluster",
            "+35% headroom -> buy ~120 TB"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flow",
          "spec": {
            "nodes": [
              "append",
              "active segment",
              "closed segments",
              "retention sweep",
              "unlink"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Retention Is Not Backup",
          "text": "A 7-day window means a bug found on day 8 is unrecoverable from Kafka. Retention defines the replay window, nothing more."
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "A Cross-Team Contract",
          "items": [
            "Producers, consumers and DR share one window",
            "Shrinking it silently shortens everyone's replay",
            "Announce changes; not a solo tuning knob"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Why Kafka Needs Retention (cont.)",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Hold This Picture",
          "text": "A log is a list of segment files. Retention is the rule set for deleting or rewriting those files."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "What A Partition Is",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "text",
          "lines": [
            "orders-3/",
            "  00000000000000368122.log   # records",
            "  00000000000000368122.index # offset->byte",
            "  00000000000000368122.timeindex",
            "  00000000000000368122.snapshot"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Filename is the base offset, zero-padded to 20 digits",
            "`.txnindex` and `.snapshot` carry transaction state",
            "One such directory per partition per broker"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The Active Segment"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Exactly one per partition; all writes append there",
            "Never eligible for deletion or compaction",
            "So a partition never shrinks to zero bytes"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "When A Segment Rolls"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Trigger",
            "Config",
            "Default"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Size",
              "`segment.bytes`",
              "1 GB"
            ],
            [
              "Age",
              "`segment.ms`",
              "7 days"
            ],
            [
              "Index full",
              "`segment.index.bytes`",
              "10 MB"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "array",
          "spec": {
            "cells": [
              "seg 0",
              "seg 368122",
              "seg 740k",
              "seg 1.1M",
              "ACTIVE"
            ],
            "caption": "partition = segments"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Whole Files Only",
          "text": "Kafka deletes segments, never individual records. That coarse granularity explains most retention surprises."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Index Files"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`.index` is sparse: one entry per `index.interval.bytes` (4096)",
            "Lookups binary-search the index, then scan forward",
            "`.timeindex` drives time retention and `offsetsForTimes()`",
            "Zero-copy `sendfile` serves `.log` straight from page cache"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Deletion Is Two-Phase"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flow",
          "spec": {
            "nodes": [
              "expired",
              "rename .deleted",
              "wait 60 s",
              "unlink"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`file.delete.delay.ms` (60000) lets in-flight reads finish",
            "Sweep runs every `log.retention.check.interval.ms` (5 min)",
            "Data lives past expiry until the next sweep"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "cont": true
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Segment Files: The Unit of Deletion",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "kafka-dump-log.sh \\",
            "  --files 00000000000000368122.log \\",
            "  --print-data-log   # offsets, keys, ts"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Segment Size Trade-Off",
          "items": [
            "1 GB segment on a slow topic = weeks of data",
            "Small segments reclaim precisely and fast",
            "But more open file handles, more frequent rolls"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The Window",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`retention.ms` — drop a segment once all its records age out",
            "Broker default is `log.retention.hours=168`",
            "`retention.ms=-1` means never expire by time"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Broker key",
            "Unit",
            "Precedence"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "`log.retention.ms`",
              "ms",
              "1st"
            ],
            [
              "`log.retention.minutes`",
              "min",
              "2nd"
            ],
            [
              "`log.retention.hours`",
              "hr",
              "3rd, default"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Whose Clock?"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "`message.timestamp.type`",
            "Clock",
            "Risk"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "`CreateTime`, default",
              "Producer",
              "Skew"
            ],
            [
              "`LogAppendTime`",
              "Broker",
              "None"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "A future-dated producer clock pins a segment for years",
            "`message.timestamp.difference.max.ms` rejects wild timestamps",
            "That guard is the cure for clock-skew retention bugs"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "Max, Not Min",
          "text": "Expiry is decided on the segment's LARGEST record timestamp — not the smallest, not the file mtime. One future record keeps every older record alive."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Why One Hour Means Eight"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flow",
          "spec": {
            "nodes": [
              "record written",
              "segment rolls",
              "records expire",
              "sweep runs",
              "delete delay"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "text",
          "lines": [
            "lifetime ~= retention.ms",
            "  + segment.ms          # roll wait",
            "  + check.interval.ms   # sweep",
            "  + file.delete.delay.ms"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Actual retention is always ≥ configured retention",
            "Cure a long tail by lowering `segment.ms`, not `retention.ms`",
            "A 1 GB segment on a trickle topic dominates the math"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Time-Based Retention",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Changing It Live"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "kafka-configs.sh --alter \\",
            "  --entity-type topics \\",
            "  --entity-name events \\",
            "  --add-config retention.ms=86400000"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Takes effect on the next check, not instantly",
            "Shrinking a window can unlink terabytes in one sweep",
            "Expect a disk I/O burst; do it off peak"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Compliance Caveat",
          "items": [
            "Time retention is best-effort deletion",
            "Never a guarantee of erasure by a deadline",
            "GDPR needs tombstones plus forced compaction"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Per Partition, Always",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`retention.bytes` caps ONE partition, not the topic",
            "Broker default `log.retention.bytes` is per-partition too",
            "`retention.bytes=-1` (default) means size never limits"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "text",
          "lines": [
            "retention.bytes = 10 GB",
            "x 12 partitions x RF 3",
            "= 360 GB cluster-wide, not 10 GB"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "The Classic Miscalculation",
          "text": "Topic footprint ≈ `retention.bytes` × partitions × replication factor. Multiply before you buy disk."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Time And Size Together"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Set",
            "Behaviour"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Only `retention.ms`",
              "Time decides"
            ],
            [
              "Only `retention.bytes`",
              "Size decides"
            ],
            [
              "Both",
              "OR — first to hit wins"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Size retention deletes the OLDEST segments first",
            "It stops once the partition is back under the cap",
            "The active segment is excluded, so caps get exceeded"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Hot Partition Skew"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "cards",
          "spec": {
            "cards": [
              {
                "title": "Hot key partition",
                "lines": [
                  "Hits `retention.bytes`",
                  "Drops data early",
                  "Short replay window"
                ]
              },
              {
                "title": "Cold partition",
                "lines": [
                  "Far under the cap",
                  "Keeps everything",
                  "Full window"
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Uneven keys make retention uneven across partitions",
            "Same topic, two very different histories",
            "Check real sizes before trusting the config"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "kafka-log-dirs.sh --describe \\",
            "  --bootstrap-server localhost:9092 \\",
            "  --topic-list events  # bytes per part"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Sizing The Cap"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`retention.bytes` ≈ (disk × 0.7) ÷ partitions per broker",
            "A reassignment temporarily doubles a partition on the target",
            "Budget that doubling or a rebalance fills the disk"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Size-Based Retention",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Which One Is The SLA?",
          "items": [
            "Size retention is the disk safety net",
            "Time retention is the business promise",
            "Set both; lean on time for correctness"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "No Quota Exists",
          "text": "Kafka does not enforce a log-dir quota. Exceed the disk and the log dir is marked offline, taking its replicas down."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "What Survives",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`cleanup.policy=compact` keeps at least the last value per key",
            "Superseded values for that key are deleted",
            "Records need a non-null key; null-keyed ones are dropped",
            "Order is preserved — compaction removes, never reorders"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Topic As Table",
          "text": "A compacted topic is a durable changelog. Replay from offset 0 and you rebuild current state, not a partial history."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Head And Tail"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "cards",
          "spec": {
            "cards": [
              {
                "title": "Head, dirty",
                "lines": [
                  "Not yet cleaned",
                  "Duplicate keys live",
                  "Active segment inside"
                ]
              },
              {
                "title": "Tail, clean",
                "lines": [
                  "At most 1 per key",
                  "Rewritten segments",
                  "Offsets have gaps"
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Offsets are never renumbered or reused",
            "Consumers must tolerate non-contiguous offsets",
            "Small segments merge into larger ones during the recopy"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "How A Pass Runs"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flow",
          "spec": {
            "nodes": [
              "scan head",
              "build key map",
              "recopy log",
              "drop stale",
              "swap segments"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Lag Knobs"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Config",
            "Default",
            "Effect"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "`min.compaction.lag.ms`",
              "0",
              "Floor on record age"
            ],
            [
              "`max.compaction.lag.ms`",
              "none",
              "Forces a dirty pass"
            ],
            [
              "`delete.retention.ms`",
              "24 h",
              "Tombstone life"
            ]
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Log Compaction: Latest Value per Key",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`max.compaction.lag.ms` is the GDPR-delete lever",
            "`compact,delete` also drops anything past `retention.ms`",
            "Use it for changelogs where ancient keys are dead weight"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Who Actually Uses It"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`__consumer_offsets` — Kafka's own offset store",
            "Streams KTable changelogs, Connect config topics",
            "Debezium schema history, cache warm-up topics"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "qa",
          "q": "Do I see every version of a key?",
          "a_bullets": [
            "Keep up with the head and you see EVERY message",
            "Lag behind and intermediate values may be gone",
            "Compaction is async and per-partition — 'latest' is eventual"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "properties",
          "lines": [
            "cleanup.policy=compact",
            "min.compaction.lag.ms=600000  # 10 min",
            "delete.retention.ms=604800000 # 7 d"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The Cleaner Threads",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`log.cleaner.enable=true` by default on every broker",
            "`log.cleaner.threads` defaults to 1 — often too few",
            "Each pass picks the partition with the highest dirty ratio"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "text",
          "lines": [
            "dirty ratio = dirty bytes / total bytes",
            "min.cleanable.dirty.ratio = 0.5",
            "# raise it: less CPU/IO, more disk"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Two Phases"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Phase",
            "Reads",
            "Result"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "1 Scan",
              "Dirty head",
              "key -> max offset"
            ],
            [
              "2 Recopy",
              "Log from start",
              "Writes survivors"
            ],
            [
              "Survivor",
              "Offset in map",
              "Or key unseen"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The Dedupe Buffer"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "array",
          "spec": {
            "cells": [
              "md5 + off",
              "24 B",
              "24 B",
              "24 B",
              "...",
              "~5M keys"
            ],
            "caption": "dedupe buffer, 128 MB"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`log.cleaner.dedupe.buffer.size` (128 MB) is shared by threads",
            "Entry = 16-byte MD5 of key + 8-byte offset = 24 B",
            "Usable = buffer × `log.cleaner.io.buffer.load.factor` (0.9)"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "text",
          "lines": [
            "128 MB x 0.9 / 24 B",
            "= ~5,000,000 keys per pass",
            "# more keys = more passes, not failure"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "The Silent Stall",
          "text": "If a SINGLE segment holds more keys than the buffer, the cleaner throws and that partition stops compacting. The broker keeps serving as if nothing broke."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "The Log Cleaner: How Compaction Runs",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Unsticking A Cleaner"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Raise `log.cleaner.dedupe.buffer.size`",
            "Add `log.cleaner.threads`",
            "Lower `segment.bytes` on the offending topic",
            "Restart the broker — dead cleaner threads never respawn"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "What To Watch"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`LogCleanerManager` `max-dirty-ratio`",
            "`time-since-last-run-ms` climbing forever",
            "`uncleanable-partitions-count` above zero",
            "`log-cleaner.log` holds the real exception"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Throttle And Safety",
          "items": [
            "`log.cleaner.io.max.bytes.per.second` caps cleaner I/O",
            "Keeps compaction from starving produce and fetch",
            "The active segment is never compacted — newest data safe"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Why This Bites Hard",
          "text": "A stalled cleaner on `__consumer_offsets` grows it without bound until commits crawl and group rebalances turn pathological."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The Delete Marker",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "A tombstone = valid key, NULL value",
            "The only way to remove a key from a compacted topic",
            "It is a real record: takes an offset, replicates normally"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "java",
          "lines": [
            "// key stays, value is null",
            "producer.send(new ProducerRecord<>(",
            "    \"users\", userId, null));"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "Null Is Not Empty",
          "text": "A 0-byte payload is a real value and does NOT delete the key. Only a true null marks a delete."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Two-Stage Removal"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flow",
          "spec": {
            "nodes": [
              "write tombstone",
              "drop old values",
              "keep marker",
              "wait window",
              "drop marker"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`delete.retention.ms` defaults to 86400000 (24 h)",
            "It runs from the compaction that cleaned that segment",
            "Pass 1 keeps the tombstone; a later pass removes it"
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "caption": {
    "text": "Your Kafka topic says 1-hour retention and the disk holds 8 hours — that is not a bug, it is `segment.ms` plus the sweep interval plus the delete delay. This field handbook walks the whole machinery: segment files as the real unit of deletion, why `retention.bytes` is per-partition (and how that turns 10 GB into 360 GB), how the log cleaner silently stalls when one segment holds more keys than its 128 MB dedupe buffer, and why a tombstone your consumer slept through leaves a phantom key forever. Ten pages of production-grade notes on the one policy that decides what data you keep.",
    "hashtags": [
